1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,300
What if it rained

2
00:00:01,340 --> 00:00:03,440
for a hundred years straight?

3
00:00:03,480 --> 00:00:04,910
The sky couldn't hold it.

4
00:00:04,950 --> 00:00:05,700
At any moment,

5
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:07,130
the whole atmosphere carries

6
00:00:07,170 --> 00:00:09,290
only about one inch of water.

7
00:00:09,330 --> 00:00:10,415
That water falls out

8
00:00:10,455 --> 00:00:12,000
in about nine days.

9
00:00:12,040 --> 00:00:13,620
So for the rain to keep going,

10
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:14,555
the oceans would have to

11
00:00:14,595 --> 00:00:15,860
lift it back up,

12
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:17,450
again and again.

13
00:00:17,490 --> 00:00:18,520
Earth already does that

14
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:20,040
about forty times a year,

15
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:20,770
which gives roughly

16
00:00:20,810 --> 00:00:22,970
three and a half feet of rain.

17
00:00:24,220 --> 00:00:25,200
Do it for a century,

18
00:00:25,240 --> 00:00:26,310
and the same water falls

19
00:00:26,350 --> 00:00:27,290
about four thousand times.

20
00:00:27,330 --> 00:00:27,705
That's around

21
00:00:27,745 --> 00:00:29,755
three hundred and forty feet deep,

22
00:00:29,795 --> 00:00:31,625
if nothing drained away.

23
00:00:33,470 --> 00:00:34,290
Rain isn't a supply.

24
00:00:34,330 --> 00:00:35,075
It's a loop,

25
00:00:35,115 --> 00:00:35,940
and the Sun runs it.

26
00:00:35,980 --> 00:00:37,580
Subscribe so you never miss

27
00:00:37,620 --> 00:00:39,430
the next what-if.
